#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:1
msgid "What to include (and exclude)"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:3
msgid ""
"It is easy to say everything must be documented in a project and often times "
"that is correct, but how can we get there, and are there things that don't "
"belong?"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:7
msgid ""
"At the top of the `src/lib.rs` or `main.rs` file in your binary project, "
"include the following attribute:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:14
msgid "Now run `cargo doc` and examine the output.  Here's a sample:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:16
msgid ""
"```text\n"
" Documenting docdemo v0.1.0 (/Users/username/docdemo)\n"
"warning: missing documentation for the crate\n"
" --> src/main.rs:1:1\n"
"  |\n"
"1 | / #![warn(missing_docs)]\n"
"2 | |\n"
"3 | | fn main() {\n"
"4 | |     println!(\"Hello, world!\");\n"
"5 | | }\n"
"  | |_^\n"
"  |\n"
"note: the lint level is defined here\n"
" --> src/main.rs:1:9\n"
"  |\n"
"1 | #![warn(missing_docs)]\n"
"  |         ^^^^^^^^^^^^\n"
"\n"
"warning: 1 warning emitted\n"
"\n"
"    Finished dev [unoptimized + debuginfo] target(s) in 2.96s\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:39
msgid ""
"As a library author, adding the lint `#![deny(missing_docs)]` is a great way "
"to ensure the project does not drift away from being documented well, and `#!"
"[warn(missing_docs)]` is a good way to move towards comprehensive "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:44
msgid "There are more lints in the upcoming chapter [Lints](../lints.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:46
msgid "Examples"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:48
msgid ""
"Of course this is contrived to be simple, but part of the power of "
"documentation is showing code that is easy to follow, rather than being "
"realistic.  Docs often take shortcuts with error handling because examples "
"can become complicated to follow with all the necessary set up required for "
"a simple example."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:53
msgid ""
"`Async` is a good example of this.  In order to execute an `async` example, "
"an executor needs to be available.  Examples will often shortcut this, and "
"leave users to figure out how to put the `async` code into their own runtime."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:57
msgid ""
"It is preferred that `unwrap()` not be used inside an example, and some of "
"the error handling components be hidden if they make the example too "
"difficult to follow."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:61
msgid ""
"````text\n"
"/// Example\n"
"/// ```rust\n"
"/// let fourtytwo = \"42\".parse::<u32>()?;\n"
"/// println!(\"{} + 10 = {}\", fourtytwo, fourtytwo+10);\n"
"/// ```\n"
"````"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:69
msgid ""
"When rustdoc wraps that in a main function, it will fail to compile because "
"the `ParseIntError` trait is not implemented.  In order to help both your "
"audience and your test suite, this example needs some additional code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:73
msgid ""
"````text\n"
"/// Example\n"
"/// ```rust\n"
"/// # fn main() -> Result<(), std::num::ParseIntError> {\n"
"/// let fortytwo = \"42\".parse::<u32>()?;\n"
"/// println!(\"{} + 10 = {}\", fortytwo, fortytwo+10);\n"
"/// #     Ok(())\n"
"/// # }\n"
"/// ```\n"
"````"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:84
msgid ""
"The example is the same on the doc page, but has that extra information "
"available to anyone trying to use your crate.  More about tests in the "
"upcoming [Documentation tests](documentation-tests.md) chapter."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:88
msgid "What to Exclude"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:90
msgid ""
"Certain parts of your public interface may be included by default in the "
"output of rustdoc.  The attribute `#[doc(hidden)]` can hide implementation "
"details to encourage idiomatic use of the crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:94
msgid ""
"For example, an internal `macro!` that makes the crate easier to implement "
"can become a footgun for users when it appears in the public documentation.  "
"An internal `Error` type may exist, and `impl` details should be hidden, as "
"detailed in the [API Guidelines](https://rust-lang.github.io/api-guidelines/"
"documentation.html#rustdoc-does-not-show-unhelpful-implementation-details-c-"
"hidden)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:99
msgid "Customizing the output"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:101
msgid ""
"It is possible to pass a custom css file to `rustdoc` and style the "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:108
msgid ""
"A good example of using this feature to create a dark theme is documented "
"[on this blog](https://blog.guillaume-gomez.fr/"
"articles/2016-09-16+Generating+doc+with+rustdoc+and+a+custom+theme).  Just "
"remember, dark theme is already included in the rustdoc output by clicking "
"on the gear icon in the upper right. Adding additional options to the themes "
"are as easy as creating a custom theme `.css` file and using the following "
"syntax:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/what-to-include.md:118
msgid ""
"Here is an example of a new theme, [Ayu](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"blob/HEAD/src/librustdoc/html/static/css/rustdoc.css#L2384-L2574)."
msgstr ""
